## Summary

**Root cause** — in `GPSTest/src/main/java/com/android/gpstest/Preferences.java`, the "Use Dark Theme" checkbox listener restarted the Settings activity with `FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK | FLAG_ACTIVITY_CLEAR_TASK` after calling `finish()`. Clearing the task destroyed `GpsTestActivity` (the only way the old-theme main screen was avoided), so pressing Back from Settings exited the app instead of returning to the main screen.

**Fix** — apply the theme to both activities while preserving the back stack:

1. `Preferences.java` — the checkbox listener now just calls `recreate()` on itself (posted to the main looper so the new preference value is persisted first). The task/back stack is no longer cleared, so `GpsTestActivity` stays alive underneath.

```java
chkDarkTheme.setOnPreferenceChangeListener(new Preference.OnPreferenceChangeListener() {
    @Override
    public boolean onPreferenceChange(Preference preference, Object newValue) {
        // Recreate this Activity so the new theme is immediately applied here. Other
        // Activities in the back stack (e.g., GpsTestActivity) check the theme preference
        // in their onResume() and recreate themselves if needed, so the back stack is preserved.
        new Handler().post(new Runnable() {
            @Override public void run() { recreate(); }
        });
        return true;
    }
});
```

2. `GpsTestActivity.java` — remembers which theme it was created with (`mUseDarkTheme`, set in `onCreate()`), and in `onResume()` recreates itself if the preference changed while it was in the background. So the user returns to the main screen and sees it in the newly selected theme.

3. `GpsTestActivity.removeGnssStatusListener()` — added null guards (`mLocationManager`/`mGnssStatusListener`), matching the other `remove*Listener()` methods, since `onResume()` can now return early before listeners are registered.

**Verification** — the Gradle build cannot run in this sandbox (no network and AGP 3.0.1 / jcenter artifacts are not in the local cache — a pre-existing environment limitation, the failure happens during dependency resolution before any compilation). I therefore verified compilation directly: `aapt` processed `AndroidManifest.xml` + all resources without errors and generated `R.java`, and `javac` compiled every source under `GPSTest/src/main/java` (including both modified files) against `android-26` and the support/play-services libraries with **0 errors**.

